  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text', gives the same answer for the following two replies. Reply 1: ```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}\nReply 2: {\"c\":3}\nThe rule requires Reply 1 to be scored as {\"b\":2} because that is the last object inside the code fence, and Reply 2 to be scored as {\"c\":3} because it is the last top-level object. But the function returns {\"c\":3} for both: for Reply 1, the fenced pool candidate is the entire fenced content '{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}', which fails to parse as a single JSON object, so the function falls back to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object {\"c\":3}. I would be wrong if the function's fenced pool were able to parse multiple objects inside a fence and return the last one, or if the documented rule were interpreted to mean only a fence containing exactly one object triggers fenced priority.",
